1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,227
You wake up on a mud roof beside the Nile.

2
00:00:03,227 --> 00:00:06,213
It's about 1300 BCE.

3
00:00:06,213 --> 00:00:09,191
There's no plumbing, no coffee, and no weekend.

4
00:00:09,191 --> 00:00:16,953
And down the road, a man with a blank scroll is already counting your grain.

5
00:00:16,953 --> 00:00:20,462
You have roughly 14 hours to survive this.

6
00:00:20,462 --> 00:00:21,366
First, let's be clear about who you are.

7
00:00:21,366 --> 00:00:24,128
You are not a pharaoh.

8
00:00:24,128 --> 00:00:26,423
You are not a priest.

9
00:00:26,423 --> 00:00:36,121
Statistically, you are a farmer, and you live in a small mud brick house in a village you will probably never leave.

10
00:00:36,121 --> 00:00:43,630
Egypt's history runs for about 3000 years, so ancient Egyptian life is not one single thing.

11
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:52,722
We're picking the New Kingdom because that's the stretch we have the most everyday evidence for.

12
00:00:52,722 --> 00:00:56,474
Houses, receipts, sick notes, arguments.

13
00:00:56,474 --> 00:00:56,724
Right.

14
00:00:56,724 --> 00:00:58,897
Get up.

15
00:00:58,897 --> 00:01:00,898
Your house is mud brick.

16
00:01:00,898 --> 00:01:02,143
That sounds miserable, and isn't.

17
00:01:02,143 --> 00:01:07,183
Nile mud mixed with straw, dried in the sun, plastered white.

18
00:01:07,183 --> 00:01:10,299
Thick walls keep the heat out.

19
00:01:10,299 --> 00:01:14,681
The windows are small and set high on purpose.

20
00:01:14,681 --> 00:01:24,585
You slept on the roof because inside is stifling, and the roof is also your kitchen, your storage, and your spare bedroom.

21
00:01:24,585 --> 00:01:30,800
You share it with more relatives than you'd like, and definitely with mice.

22
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:35,706
Clothing is linen because Egypt grew flax, and linen breathes.

23
00:01:35,706 --> 00:01:37,176
Men wear a kilt.

24
00:01:37,176 --> 00:01:39,028
Women wear a straight dress.

25
00:01:39,028 --> 00:01:43,186
Small children often wore nothing at all, and nobody thought twice about it.

26
00:01:43,186 --> 00:01:44,022
Now, makeup.

27
00:01:44,022 --> 00:01:46,709
Nearly everyone wore eye paint, not just the rich.

28
00:01:46,709 --> 00:01:49,467
It's mostly ground galena, a dark lead ore.

29
00:01:49,467 --> 00:01:53,417
It cut the glare like an athlete's under-eye paint.

30
00:01:53,417 --> 00:01:57,314
Two of the lead compounds in Egyptian kohl don't occur naturally in Egypt.

31
00:01:57,314 --> 00:01:59,653
Somebody was manufacturing them deliberately, over weeks.

32
00:01:59,653 --> 00:02:05,180
One lab study found those compounds boost an immune signal in cultured skin cells.

33
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:09,464
That's a cell study, not proof it protected real eyes.

34
00:02:09,464 --> 00:02:10,893
Hold it loosely.

35
00:02:10,893 --> 00:02:13,310
Breakfast is bread and beer.

36
00:02:13,310 --> 00:02:15,065
So is lunch.

37
00:02:15,065 --> 00:02:17,531
So, mostly, is dinner.

38
00:02:17,531 --> 00:02:24,169
Egyptian beer was thick and filling, closer to a soupy porridge than a lager.

39
00:02:24,169 --> 00:02:30,155
Add onions, dates, cucumbers, river fish, and, on festival days, actual meat.

40
00:02:30,155 --> 00:02:31,415
The bread is the problem.

41
00:02:31,415 --> 00:02:38,053
Grain was ground on stone, and the stone shed grit into the flour.

42
00:02:38,053 --> 00:02:44,340
Egyptian skulls show teeth worn flat, nerves exposed, abscesses in the jaw.

43
00:02:44,340 --> 00:02:46,152
That is not a theory.

44
00:02:46,152 --> 00:02:48,135
You can look at the skulls.

45
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:51,940
Unfortunately, that created another problem.

46
00:02:52,290 --> 00:02:54,059
There were no dentists.

47
00:02:54,059 --> 00:02:57,942
Outside, everything depends on the river.

48
00:02:57,942 --> 00:03:04,515
Once a year, the Nile flooded and dropped a layer of black silt across the fields.

49
00:03:04,515 --> 00:03:08,711
That silt is the entire reason Egypt existed.

50
00:03:08,711 --> 00:03:15,788
Egyptians called their country the Black Land, after the mud, not after the sand.

51
00:03:15,788 --> 00:03:23,099
So your year has three seasons, the flood, the growing, and the harvest.

52
00:03:23,099 --> 00:03:31,918
During the flood, your fields are underwater, so the state may put you to work on building projects instead.

53
00:03:31,918 --> 00:03:34,193
That is not slavery.

54
00:03:34,193 --> 00:03:42,450
It's closer to conscripted labor, and the workers were fed, housed, and written down by name.

55
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:45,710
Which brings us to the pyramids, briefly.

56
00:03:45,710 --> 00:03:51,941
The people who built them had job titles, ration lists, and their own cemetery on site.

57
00:03:51,941 --> 00:03:54,468
They ate bread, beer, and a surprising amount of beef.

58
00:03:54,468 --> 00:04:00,363
And by your day in 1300 BCE, the Great Pyramid is already over a thousand years old.

59
00:04:00,363 --> 00:04:05,199
It's as ancient to you as Charlemagne is to a modern viewer.

60
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:06,443
Back to work.

61
00:04:06,443 --> 00:04:13,207
You dig channels, haul water, and watch the flood level obsessively, because flood height sets your taxes.

62
00:04:13,207 --> 00:04:14,381
Too low?

63
00:04:14,381 --> 00:04:15,397
Famine.

64
00:04:15,397 --> 00:04:17,619
Too high?

65
00:04:17,619 --> 00:04:17,869
Your village is underwater.

66
00:04:17,869 --> 00:04:19,590
And this is where it gets bleak.

67
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:25,252
Egypt was a bureaucracy first and a civilization second.

68
00:04:25,252 --> 00:04:34,295
Scribes measured your field, estimated your yield, and claimed a share of the grain before you'd harvested it.

69
00:04:34,295 --> 00:04:39,557
Tomb paintings show farmers being dragged in front of assessors.

70
00:04:39,557 --> 00:04:43,651
Nobody painted that as a scandal.

71
00:04:43,651 --> 00:04:45,833
It was just Tuesday.

72
00:04:45,833 --> 00:04:55,677
Above you sit builders, craftspeople, soldiers, priests, and, at the top of everything that mattered, scribes.

73
00:04:55,677 --> 00:04:57,985
A scribe could read.

74
00:04:57,985 --> 00:05:00,374
A scribe lifted nothing.

75
00:05:00,374 --> 00:05:02,720
A scribe paid less.

76
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:18,300
Which is why Egyptian schoolboys were handed a text that runs through every other trade, the potter, the fisherman, the metalworker, and describes each one as filthy and exhausting.

77
00:05:18,650 --> 00:05:23,101
It is ancient careers propaganda, and it worked.

78
00:05:23,101 --> 00:05:24,427
Now, religion.

79
00:05:24,427 --> 00:05:29,034
Forget the enormous temples for a moment.

80
00:05:29,034 --> 00:05:32,485
You were probably never allowed inside one.

81
00:05:32,485 --> 00:05:36,796
Those were the god's house, staffed by priests, closed to the public.

82
00:05:36,796 --> 00:05:48,919
Public religion reached you at festivals, when a god was carried out through the streets in a boat, and the whole town stopped working.

83
00:05:48,919 --> 00:05:52,055
Your actual religion happened at home.

84
00:05:52,055 --> 00:06:05,320
On a shelf in your front room sat Bes, a snarling dwarf god with his tongue out, and Tawaret, a pregnant hippopotamus standing upright with a crocodile down her back.

85
00:06:05,670 --> 00:06:11,267
Egypt took its two most dangerous animals and reassigned them as babysitters.

86
00:06:11,267 --> 00:06:19,379
You'd also keep painted busts of your own dead relatives up there and leave them food.

87
00:06:19,379 --> 00:06:24,585
Egyptians were also fanatically clean.

88
00:06:24,585 --> 00:06:31,480
People washed constantly and used a paste of natron and oil instead of soap.

89
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:34,104
Shaved heads beat lice.

90
00:06:34,104 --> 00:06:36,232
Wigs went on top, if you could afford one.

91
00:06:36,232 --> 00:06:40,393
Magic and medicine were the same job.

92
00:06:40,393 --> 00:06:49,961
Egyptian doctors set bones, stitched wounds, and packed injuries with honey, which genuinely is antibacterial.

93
00:06:49,961 --> 00:06:52,910
They also prescribed spells.

94
00:06:53,260 --> 00:07:02,897
One surviving surgical text works down the body injury by injury and sorts each case into one to treat, one to fight, and one not to treat at all.

95
00:07:02,897 --> 00:07:06,623
That last category is a doctor writing down when to stop.

96
00:07:06,623 --> 00:07:09,415
But this is where things get strange.

97
00:07:09,415 --> 00:07:12,374
Your real enemies are invisible.

98
00:07:12,374 --> 00:07:16,888
The river carried parasites, and mummies still test positive for them.

99
00:07:16,888 --> 00:07:20,839
Dust wrecked lungs, and small cuts turned septic.

100
00:07:20,839 --> 00:07:25,330
And if you think that sounds unpleasant, wait until childbirth.

101
00:07:25,330 --> 00:07:31,850
Childbirth was the most dangerous thing that happened in an Egyptian village.

102
00:07:32,200 --> 00:07:36,655
Women gave birth crouching or kneeling, sometimes supported on painted bricks.

103
00:07:36,655 --> 00:07:41,725
Archaeologists found one at Abydos, decorated with protective images for the mother.

104
00:07:41,725 --> 00:07:45,682
Many women didn't survive it, and neither did many small children.

105
00:07:45,682 --> 00:07:48,207
Marriage, though, is oddly familiar.

106
00:07:48,207 --> 00:07:52,497
As far as we can tell, there was no ceremony and no religious rite.

107
00:07:52,497 --> 00:07:55,589
You moved in together, and you sorted out the property.

108
00:07:55,589 --> 00:07:58,729
Egyptians described it as founding a household.

109
00:07:58,729 --> 00:08:01,705
And here's the fact that surprises people most.

110
00:08:01,705 --> 00:08:04,910
In legal documents, Egyptian women own property.

111
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:14,951
They inherit it, sell it, sue over it, testify in court, and start divorces, under their own names, without a male guardian signing for them.

112
00:08:14,951 --> 00:08:18,561
That was not standard elsewhere in the ancient world.

113
00:08:18,561 --> 00:08:20,412
Be careful with it, though.

114
00:08:20,412 --> 00:08:22,851
Legal rights are not equality.

115
00:08:22,851 --> 00:08:24,873
Rations went to men.

116
00:08:24,873 --> 00:08:28,473
Our surviving texts lean literate and comfortable.

117
00:08:28,473 --> 00:08:30,750
A woman could take you to court.

118
00:08:30,750 --> 00:08:32,971
She could not take your job.

119
00:08:32,971 --> 00:08:35,653
Education was for a minority.

120
00:08:35,653 --> 00:08:41,915
Boys aimed at administration learned to write by copying texts onto limestone flakes.

121
00:08:41,915 --> 00:08:45,640
We still have their homework, with the teacher's corrections on it.

122
00:08:45,990 --> 00:08:51,869
For wealthy Egyptians, the situation was completely different.

123
00:08:51,869 --> 00:08:53,405
Nowhere more than in death.

124
00:08:53,405 --> 00:09:02,107
The full mummification everyone pictures, 70 days, natron, linen, a painted coffin, was expensive.

125
00:09:02,107 --> 00:09:13,465
Ordinary people got a stripped down version, or a shallow grave at the desert's edge, where dry sand did the preserving for free.

126
00:09:13,465 --> 00:09:19,286
Which is how Egyptians learned that the desert preserves bodies in the first place.

127
00:09:19,286 --> 00:09:22,710
What everyone shared was the belief.

128
00:09:22,710 --> 00:09:33,080
You needed your body intact, your name spoken aloud, and food left at your grave, or you stopped continuing.

129
00:09:33,430 --> 00:09:38,647
Which is why feeding those busts on your shelf mattered.

130
00:09:38,647 --> 00:09:40,143
It wasn't sentiment.

131
00:09:40,143 --> 00:09:41,107
It was maintenance.

132
00:09:41,107 --> 00:09:42,324
And yes, the cats.

133
00:09:42,324 --> 00:09:50,135
Cats were genuinely valued in your lifetime, mostly because they killed the rats and snakes going after your grain.

134
00:09:50,135 --> 00:10:01,019
But the famous mummified cats came much later, a late-period industry where kittens were bred and killed to be sold to pilgrims as offerings.

135
00:10:01,019 --> 00:10:05,403
That's roughly 700 years after your morning.

136
00:10:05,403 --> 00:10:07,149
Egypt changed.

137
00:10:07,149 --> 00:10:11,344
So, 14 hours later, you climb back up to the roof.

138
00:10:11,344 --> 00:10:15,170
You are sunburnt, taxed, and slightly toothless.

139
00:10:15,520 --> 00:10:22,661
But you had bread, a family, a river that came back every year, and a hippo goddess watching your door.

140
00:10:22,661 --> 00:10:29,085
We remember Egypt through its funerals, because funerals are what survived.

141
00:10:29,085 --> 00:10:31,385
The tombs are the exception.

142
00:10:31,385 --> 00:10:33,057
You are the rule.

143
00:10:33,057 --> 00:10:35,797
They complained about their boss.

144
00:10:35,797 --> 00:10:38,013
They worried about money.

145
00:10:38,013 --> 00:10:41,717
They wanted their kids to have it easier.

146
00:10:41,717 --> 00:10:46,670
If you'd like to wake up somewhere else next time, you know what to do.
